    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Wed, Jul 16, 2014 at 10:21 PM,  &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">gregor...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; I am using ggplot to create maps for visualizing the output of a predictive\r<br>&gt; model for spatial data. I would like to visualize the mean and variance\r<br>&gt; (error) of the data simultaneously. One method for doing this would be to\r<br>&gt; use an HSL model for my scale_color_gradientn() where hue is mapped to the\r<br>&gt; mean and either saturation or luminance is mapped to variance. Is it\r<br>&gt; possible to map the aspects of a color to different variables in ggplot?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; For the sake of a minimal working example, I&#39;ve borrowed from the\r<br>&gt; documentation for scale_colour_gradientn().\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; library(ggplot2)\r<br>&gt; library(RColorBrewer)\r<br>&gt; dsub &lt;- subset(diamonds, x &gt; 5 &amp; x &lt; 6 &amp; y &gt; 5 &amp; y &lt; 6)\r<br>&gt; dsub &lt;- mutate(dsub, var=x, diff=sqrt(abs(x-y))* sign(x-y))\r<br>&gt; d &lt;- qplot(x, y, data=dsub, colour=diff)\r<br>&gt; d + scale_color_gradientn(colours=brewer.pal(7,&quot;YlOrRd&quot;))\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Note that there is now a variable &quot;var&quot; in dsub, which I&#39;d like to map to\r<br>&gt; luminance or saturation, or otherwise visualize simultaneously with diff.\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "You can change the color space within scale_color_gradientn() with the\r<br>space = argument, but you cannot map more than one variable to the\r<br>same aesthetic, which it appears you are trying to do. In other words,\r<br>you can&#39;t map color to diff for one purpose and then color to x for\r<br>another purpose. If you can figure out a way to use a fill aesthetic\r<br>for one of them, you might be in business.\r<br>\r<br>Dennis\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Cheers,\r<br>&gt; Gregory\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; --\r<br>&gt; --\r<br>&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the ggplot2 mailing\r<br>&gt; list.\r<br>&gt; Please provide a reproducible example:\r<br>&gt; <a href=\"https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370474212000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0_h02viSlSAtUuUYMMLZpE\">https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility</a>\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; To post: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a>\r<br>&gt; To unsubscribe: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a>\r<br>&gt; More options: <a href=\"http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370474212000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3Sk6Apb8T1O-ntAzGVpztT\">http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2</a>\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; ---\r<br>&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups\r<br>&gt; &quot;ggplot2&quot; group.\r<br>&gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an\r<br>&gt; email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt; For more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370474212000&amp;usg=AOvVaw16DzyeisqG61dKXiyK4IM0\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.\r<br>"
    ]
