  {
   "id": "qQ1v8IuSkM8J",
   "from": "JiHO",
   "author": "fc22cb727156ff4f",
   "date": "2014-07-19T22:32:16Z",
   "subject": "Re: Simultaneous visualization of mean and error in spatial data? (with example)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "It seems we use ggplot for similar purposes. I&#39;ve been wanting to do\r<br>exactly what you want for a similar purpose (mapping model results).\r<br>There might even be a question about it in the archive of this mailing\r<br>list.\r<br>\r<br>Unfortunately, there is a scale_fill_hue but not a\r<br>scale_fill_saturation. Writing it would make for a very nice addition\r<br>to ggplot but I could never figure out where to start. In addition,\r<br>ideally, the legend should be bidimensional, with hue on one axis and\r<br>saturation on the other. This is likely to be even harder.\r<br>\r<br>The workarounds I explored were:\r<br>- mapping the result to color and the uncertainty to transparency\r<br>(alpha) making more uncertain regions more transparent, i.e. less\r<br>visible.\r<br>- creating a layer with colour mapped to the result and create another\r<br>layer with a solid grey fill but alpha mapped to confidence (1 -\r<br>uncertainty). The colour gradient should have the same saturation and\r<br>luminance, and vary in hue only (that&#39;s the case for the default hue\r<br>scale I think). The grey of the layer above should have the same\r<br>luminance as the colours (i.e. mimic the desaturation of one of the\r<br>colours). The transparency should be full for the points of high\r<br>confidence. Doing it this way, the uncertain regions are grey or\r<br>greyish and the well predicted regions should full colours.\r<br>\r<br>Hope that helps.\r<br>\r<br>JiHO\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>On 7/17/14, Dennis Murphy &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">djm...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; On Wed, Jul 16, 2014 at 10:21 PM,  &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">gregor...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt;&gt; I am using ggplot to create maps for visualizing the output of a\r<br>&gt;&gt; predictive\r<br>&gt;&gt; model for spatial data. I would like to visualize the mean and variance\r<br>&gt;&gt; (error) of the data simultaneously. One method for doing this would be to\r<br>&gt;&gt; use an HSL model for my scale_color_gradientn() where hue is mapped to\r<br>&gt;&gt; the\r<br>&gt;&gt; mean and either saturation or luminance is mapped to variance. Is it\r<br>&gt;&gt; possible to map the aspects of a color to different variables in ggplot?\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; For the sake of a minimal working example, I&#39;ve borrowed from the\r<br>&gt;&gt; documentation for scale_colour_gradientn().\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; library(ggplot2)\r<br>&gt;&gt; library(RColorBrewer)\r<br>&gt;&gt; dsub &lt;- subset(diamonds, x &gt; 5 &amp; x &lt; 6 &amp; y &gt; 5 &amp; y &lt; 6)\r<br>&gt;&gt; dsub &lt;- mutate(dsub, var=x, diff=sqrt(abs(x-y))* sign(x-y))\r<br>&gt;&gt; d &lt;- qplot(x, y, data=dsub, colour=diff)\r<br>&gt;&gt; d + scale_color_gradientn(colours=brewer.pal(7,&quot;YlOrRd&quot;))\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; Note that there is now a variable &quot;var&quot; in dsub, which I&#39;d like to map to\r<br>&gt;&gt; luminance or saturation, or otherwise visualize simultaneously with diff.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; You can change the color space within scale_color_gradientn() with the\r<br>&gt; space = argument, but you cannot map more than one variable to the\r<br>&gt; same aesthetic, which it appears you are trying to do. In other words,\r<br>&gt; you can&#39;t map color to diff for one purpose and then color to x for\r<br>&gt; another purpose. If you can figure out a way to use a fill aesthetic\r<br>&gt; for one of them, you might be in business.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Dennis\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; Cheers,\r<br>&gt;&gt; Gregory\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; --\r<br>&gt;&gt; --\r<br>&gt;&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the ggplot2\r<br>&gt;&gt; mailing\r<br>&gt;&gt; list.\r<br>&gt;&gt; Please provide a reproducible example:\r<br>&gt;&gt; <a href=\"https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370474213000&amp;usg=AOvVaw34jU9belrb1Hw2I3QDevTH\">https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility</a>\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; To post: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a>\r<br>&gt;&gt; To unsubscribe: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a>\r<br>&gt;&gt; More options: <a href=\"http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370474213000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1PVRtDDqD9hCl0YQQekehq\">http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2</a>\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; ---\r<br>&gt;&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups\r<br>&gt;&gt; &quot;ggplot2&quot; group.\r<br>&gt;&gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an\r<br>&gt;&gt; email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt;&gt; For more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370474213000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2UCOELzZpYTzrWJ--y8Hly\">https://groups.google.com/d/op